methods.explanation:
The exponent-sum rule lets us write every term using the common factor \(2^n\): \(2^{n+4}=16\cdot2^n\) and \(2^{n+3}=8\cdot2^n\). Therefore
\[
\frac{2^{n+4}-2(2^n)}{2(2^{n+3})}
=\frac{(16-2)2^n}{16\cdot2^n}
=\frac{14}{16}
=\frac78.
\]
The cancellation is valid because \(2^n>0\) for every real \(n\). The answer is \(\boxed{\frac78}\), choice \(\boxed{\text{D}}\).
